0:{"buildId":"EmVKRky_4Ersv6lEjP4pJ","data":[{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","$L2",null,{"post":{"slug":"rest-day-active-recovery","category":"RECOVERY","title":"What to do on a rest day: active recovery ideas","description":"Learn how walking, easy mobility and light activity can fit into a rest day without turning recovery into another hard workout.","lead":"A rest day can be completely restful or lightly active — the goal is to support the training that comes next.","keywords":["rest day","active recovery","recovery day workout","what to do on rest day"],"stage":12,"activity":"walk","takeaway":"Recovery is successful when it helps you return ready for useful training.","sections":[{"heading":"Rest can mean actual rest","body":"You do not need to earn recovery by adding another workout. If you are tired, busy or carrying fatigue from training, a quiet day can be the right choice."},{"heading":"Use easy movement when it feels good","body":"Walking, relaxed cycling or a gentle mobility flow can provide movement without the goal of setting records. Keep the effort low enough that it feels restorative."},{"heading":"Avoid turning recovery into competition","body":"A recovery session does not need high heart-rate targets, difficult circuits or a long checklist. If it feels like another hard training day, it is probably serving a different purpose."},{"heading":"Look at the whole week","body":"Rest days make more sense when viewed next to your harder sessions. Place recovery where it helps you protect the quality and consistency of the training you care about most."}]},"index":11,"total":14,"prevHref":"/blog/morning-mobility-routine","nextHref":"/blog/workout-motivation-without-motivation"}],[["$","script","script-0",{"src":"/_next/static/chunks/1f3e3iqq5mikc.js","async":true}]],["$","$L3",null,{"children":["$","$4",null,{"name":"Next.MetadataOutlet","children":"$@5"}]}]]}],"isPartial":"$@6","staleTime":"$7","varyParams":null},{"rsc":["$","$1","h",{"children":[nu